{"parentUuid":"ea327f68-4ac9-4972-87fe-57324a2731b3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UAYc2cJU5LgtV14arqiasv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Got it — this is definitive. The smoking gun is in `codex.mjs`:\n\n```js\n// lib/codex.mjs:63\nfunction buildThreadParams(cwd, options = {}) {\n  return {\n    ...\n    ephemeral: options.ephemeral ?? true,  // DEFAULT IS EPHEMERAL\n    ...\n  };\n}\n\n// lib/codex.mjs:919 — review path\nephemeral: true,              // hardcoded\n\n// lib/codex.mjs:986 — task/turn path (what adversarial-review uses)\nephemeral: options.persistThread ? false : true,  // defaults to ephemeral\n```\n\nAnd the adversarial-review command routes through `runAppServerTurn` (`codex-companion.mjs:408`), which hits the `ephemeral: true` default.\n\nSo the plugin uses the **Codex app-server protocol** (not `codex exec`), and every adversarial-review thread is ephemeral by design. That's consistent with every piece of evidence we have:\n\n- **No rollout files anywhere, not just for the failed sessions.** Ephemeral threads don't write to `~/.codex/sessions/`. Period. The missing directory isn't a clue about failure — it's the normal state for this command.\n- **Three identical microsecond fingerprints.** All three runs went through the same ephemeral path. SessionStart and UserPromptSubmit fired with `transcript_path: null`, our turn-start handler proceeded anyway, and Stop fired but hit our `lifecycle.go:322-324` bail-on-empty-SessionRef guard.\n- **The timeout is real but mostly incidental here.** Even if the plugin had completed cleanly in 2 minutes, the session would still have been stuck ACTIVE, because ephemeral means no transcript to save.\n\n## What this means for the plan\n\n**Revised culprit ranking:**\n1. **Ephemeral mode + our bail-on-empty-transcript** — the primary bug. Ships every time adversarial-review runs.\n2. **10m Bash-tool timeout SIGKILL** — real but only matters for runs that actually exceed the timeout; the ephemeral issue hits every run regardless.\n\n**Don't file with Codex.** Ephemeral is documented, working-as-designed, and intentional — the whole point of the app-server protocol is to support short-lived review threads that don't pollute the user's session history. The plugin is also doing the right thing by making reviews ephemeral. The bug is entirely on our side.\n\n**Revised Q2 fix:** the rollout-reconciliation idea I pitched earlier is useless here — there's no rollout to reconcile. Two real options:\n\n1. **Accept ephemeral sessions as first-class.** Change the turn-start/turn-end handlers so null transcript_path is valid. Capture what the hook payloads give us directly: prompt from UserPromptSubmit, `last_assistant_message` from Stop, turn IDs from both. No transcript file means no full transcript capture, but we'd at least produce a proper checkpoint with prompt + summary, transition phase cleanly, and avoid the stuck-ACTIVE + stub-metadata outcome.\n2. **Refuse ephemeral sessions at SessionStart.** Log a warning (\"Entire doesn't support ephemeral Codex sessions; this turn will not be captured\") and don't register the session at all. Clean, but the user loses any record of their adversarial-review runs.\n\nI'd lean #1 because adversarial-review is a legitimate workflow that users will want captured — even partial capture (prompt + final message + files_touched) beats nothing. And it's not that hard: we already have the structured data from hook payloads; we just currently ignore everything after the SessionRef check fails.\n\n**Q1 guards still stand** and become *more* important under #1 — if we're accepting ephemeral sessions, the \"don't write stub metadata.json when there's no transcript AND StepCount==0\" guard is what keeps us from producing empty checkpoints when things still go sideways.\n\nWant me to scope this as a single PR: (a) Q1 guards, (b) accept ephemeral turn-end with partial-capture path